::ENG4_U06_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.C.03] Multimedia scenario: A senior project team will publish a multimedia text about youth financial literacy using a written introduction, infographic, narration, source credits, and a shareable digital format. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
~A. Add more media elements even if they repeat the same idea.#Incorrect. Extra media is not automatically stronger. Each mode should add a clear purpose or support audience understanding in a new way.
=B. Match each media element to a clear purpose and make sure all sources are credited.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~C. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Multimedia texts still require revision. Students should check accuracy, access, source credits, and coherence before publishing.
~D. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Borrowed visuals require credit. Ethical publication depends on acknowledging outside media and respecting source use.
}

::ENG4_U06_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Multimedia scenario: A senior project team will publish a multimedia text about youth financial literacy using a written introduction, infographic, narration, source credits, and a shareable digital format. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
~A. Add more media elements even if they repeat the same idea.#Incorrect. Extra media is not automatically stronger. Each mode should add a clear purpose or support audience understanding in a new way.
~B. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Multimedia texts still require revision. Students should check accuracy, access, source credits, and coherence before publishing.
~C. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Borrowed visuals require credit. Ethical publication depends on acknowledging outside media and respecting source use.
=D. Use the infographic to show budgeting mistakes visually while narration explains their significance.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
}

::ENG4_U06_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Multimedia scenario: A senior project team will publish a multimedia text about youth financial literacy using a written introduction, infographic, narration, source credits, and a shareable digital format. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
=A. Choose a shareable format that preserves readable visuals, clear audio, and source credits.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. Add more media elements even if they repeat the same idea.#Incorrect. Extra media is not automatically stronger. Each mode should add a clear purpose or support audience understanding in a new way.
~C. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Multimedia texts still require revision. Students should check accuracy, access, source credits, and coherence before publishing.
~D. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Borrowed visuals require credit. Ethical publication depends on acknowledging outside media and respecting source use.
}

::ENG4_U06_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Multimedia scenario: A senior project team will publish a multimedia text about youth financial literacy using a written introduction, infographic, narration, source credits, and a shareable digital format. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
~A. Add more media elements even if they repeat the same idea.#Incorrect. Extra media is not automatically stronger. Each mode should add a clear purpose or support audience understanding in a new way.
~B. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Multimedia texts still require revision. Students should check accuracy, access, source credits, and coherence before publishing.
=C. Revise the text, visuals, narration, and credits so the final product is accurate and accessible.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~D. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Borrowed visuals require credit. Ethical publication depends on acknowledging outside media and respecting source use.
}

